# Example environment file for local secrets.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the real values, then `source .env`.
# `.env` is gitignored (`.gitignore:59-61`) and must never be committed; this
# `.example` carries no value and is the shape, so it is safe to commit and it
# is the one name the ignore rule lets back in.
#
# Nothing in this repository reads these variables at build or test time. The
# compiler does not know they exist. They are here for three callers only: a
# `wrangler` run started by hand from this machine, the documentation of what
# the deploy workflow expects to find in GitHub's secret store, and
# `docs/platforms/windows/power-on.py`, which signs in to the appOnFly portal
# to turn the Windows box on.

# ---- Cloudflare ----
# The site's deploy path is a Cloudflare Pages project that wrangler uploads to.
# The architecture and the whole procedure live in `site/README.md` § Deployment,
# which is their only home; what follows is only what a key needs said next to it.
#
# Use a CUSTOM API token, never the Global API Key: that one covers the whole
# account and cannot be revoked selectively, so losing it costs everything at
# once instead of one project.
#
# For a deploy that stops at `pages.dev`, ONE policy is enough:
#
#   Policy 1 - resource: Entire Account
#     Developer Platform -> Cloudflare Pages   Edit   create the project, deploy
#     Account & Billing  -> Account Settings   Read   read the account id
#
# heroes-lang.org is on Cloudflare now, and its zone takes a second policy. The
# trap is that the PERMISSION LIST ITSELF CHANGES with the resource selector:
# under "Entire Account" there is no Zone, no DNS and no Zone Settings entry at
# all, and the things it does offer under "DNS & Zones" (Account DNS Settings,
# DNS Firewall, DNS View, Registrar) are none of what is needed.
#
#   Policy 2 - resource: All Domains
#     Zone                                     Edit   create zones, read assigned NS
#     DNS                                      Edit   records
#     Zone Settings                            Edit   SSL/TLS, Always Use HTTPS
#
# All Domains rather than Specified Domains: `Zone: Edit` across every domain of
# the account is what grants `zone.create`, and at the start the zone does not
# exist to be selected.
#
# Left out on purpose: Email Routing, which can reroute the domain's mail, and
# Account Rulesets. Cloudflare Web Analytics has no token permission at all.
#
# Give the token a short expiry. A token that outlives the work it was made for
# is a key left in a door, and the expiry is the one protection that does not
# depend on anybody remembering.
#
# One trap worth an hour: the current builder issues ACCOUNT-OWNED tokens, the
# ones with a `cfat_` prefix, and `/user/tokens/verify` answers `Invalid API
# Token` for every one of them, because that endpoint is user-scoped and these
# tokens belong to the account. Verify at
# `/accounts/<account_id>/tokens/verify` instead. Every other endpoint takes the
# token as it is.
#
# Same variable name the deploy workflow expects as a repository secret, so that
# the local invocation and the CI one cannot drift apart.
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-cloudflare-api-token-here

# The account that owns the Pages project. wrangler infers it from the token
# when the token reaches exactly one account; the workflow and any direct API
# call want it stated. It is an identifier rather than a credential, and it is
# still only written here, because a file that is half secret is a file people
# stop reading carefully.
export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=your-cloudflare-account-id-here

# ---- Anthropic ----
# For measuring `spec/heroes-spec.md` against the tokeniser that actually reads
# it. `heroes measure` answers today from two VENDORED instruments and takes the
# larger, and design.md §1.6 says the honest thing about that in its own words:
# the reader's real tokeniser is unpublished, so the spread applies to the
# ceiling too. This key is how that number stops being unpublished.
#
# `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`, which returns a count and runs no inference.
# Headers `x-api-key`, `anthropic-version`, `content-type: application/json`; the
# body is a model id and one user message holding the file's text.
#
# **A count is only a measurement together with the model that produced it**, so
# the model id is pinned in the repository beside the number, never here: an
# environment variable that quietly changes which tokeniser answered is a
# premise that expires without telling anybody.
#
# The name is the SDKs' and the `ant` CLI's, so nothing needs wiring and the
# local name cannot drift from a CI one. Use a WORKSPACE-scoped key, never an
# Admin key (`sk-ant-admin…`), which reaches members and billing and is not
# needed to count anything. Give it a short expiry, as above.
#
# Nothing in this repository reads it yet, and what would is a `heroes` flag
# (§10) that REFRESHES a recorded number rather than a check that needs the
# network to pass. That is a panel's decision, not this file's.
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-anthropic-api-key-here

# ---- Heroes held-out keys ----
# Three keys, three archives (tests/thesis/held-out/README.md): the suite, the
# reserve, and the held-out runs' transcripts. Each is 32 random bytes,
# `openssl rand -hex 32`, never written on a command line. The grader refuses
# to run a model's program while any of them is set in its environment.
export HEROES_TASKS_KEY=your-first-64-char-hex-value
export HEROES_RESERVE_KEY=your-second-64-char-hex-value
export HEROES_RUNS_KEY=your-third-64-char-hex-value

# ---- appOnFly (the Windows box) ----
# `docs/platforms/windows/power-on.py` turns the project's Windows VPS on by
# signing in to the appOnFly customer portal over its own HTTPS API, with no
# browser (docs/platforms/windows/WINDOWS-MACHINE.md is the machine). It needs
# the portal account's email and password, and nothing else: the account's
# subscription, virtual machine and auto-shutdown timer are all discovered at
# run time, so changing to a different appOnFly account means changing only
# these two values.
#
# The email names the account. It is personal rather than secret, and it is
# still written here, because a file that is half secret is a file people stop
# reading carefully (the same reason the Cloudflare account id is above).
export APPONFLY_EMAIL=you@example.com

# The password. PREFER the macOS login keychain, which keeps it off disk
# entirely:
#
#   security add-generic-password -s apponfly-portal -a you@example.com -w
#
# power-on.py reads that keychain item FIRST (service `apponfly-portal`,
# account the email above) and only falls back to this variable. So if you use
# the keychain, leave the placeholder below as it is. Set a real value here
# only when you are not using the keychain; `.env` is gitignored, but a
# password on disk is still a password on disk.
export APPONFLY_PASSWORD=your-apponfly-portal-password-here

